WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.605 --> 00:00:12.730
LEIGHA JARETT: Hi, and
welcome to our session where

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:15.280
we're going under the hood
for Google Cloud's data

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:18.340
technologies.

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:20.170
I'm Leigha Jarett,
and I'm a developer

00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:23.800
advocate here at Google Cloud,
and I focus on data analytics.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:26.620
But today, I'm also joined
by my colleague, Derek,

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:30.880
who focuses on
operational databases.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:34.420
Today, we're going to be
talking about two technologies.

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:36.850
First, Spanner on
the operational

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:40.450
databases side of things on the
left-hand side of our screen.

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:42.670
And then, we're
also going to focus

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:46.850
on BigQuery, our data warehouse
for analytical processing.

00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:49.900
And these are just a few of the
pieces in the broader Google

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:51.940
Cloud data ecosystem.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:54.220
Integrations between
both of these products

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.500
and other products
that support data

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:00.100
fabric, artificial intelligence,
and business intelligence

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:03.910
all go into making it simple for
data practitioners and business

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:09.430
users to power data-driven
innovation using Google Cloud.

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:11.680
So the story of both
Spanner and BigQuery

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:14.410
starts with the evolution
of the separation

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:16.300
of storage and compute.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.370
Historically,
database systems have

00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:21.790
been architected with tightly
coupled storage and compute,

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:24.370
so there's a dependence on
locally attached storage

00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:26.680
because attaching
storage to a network

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:29.450
would result in higher latency.

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:31.370
But more recently,
these constraints

00:01:31.370 --> 00:01:34.460
have been lifted, largely
due to faster networks,

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:37.230
which we're going to
talk about momentarily.

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:40.320
So now, with architecture
like BigQuery and Spanner,

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:43.790
we have a separation
of storage and compute,

00:01:43.790 --> 00:01:45.740
and this allows us
to seamlessly scale

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:48.080
to meet your high
throughput data needs.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:50.240
New compute nodes
can easily be added

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:52.220
to handle large
workloads, and we

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:55.730
don't need to rebalance the data
and ensure that a local replica

00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:57.870
is stored on new nodes.

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:01.070
So let's take a closer look
at how our distributed storage

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:04.370
system, Colossus, is
also faster, reliable,

00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:08.080
and cost-effective for you.

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:10.289
So Colossus is Google's
next generation

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:12.300
distributed file system.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:15.240
It manages, stores,
and provides access

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:19.110
to all of your data for
both BigQuery and Spanner.

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:21.900
Its design enhances
storage scalability

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:26.790
and improves availability
to handle the massive growth

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:28.620
in data needs.

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:31.590
Colossus introduced a
distributed metadata model

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:36.100
that delivers a more scalable
and highly available system.

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:38.110
With all this data
stored remotely,

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:41.500
if a single node fails,
there's no data lost, or even

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:44.020
made temporarily unavailable.

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:47.200
Not to mention, remote storage
is dramatically cheaper

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:49.600
than local storage.

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:51.010
And the proof is in the picture.

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:54.340
So not only does Colossus
handle your first-party data

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:56.590
stored in Google
Cloud, but it also

00:02:56.590 --> 00:03:00.100
stores the information needed
to run vital Google systems that

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:05.030
are used by so many
people all over the world.

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:07.070
Like we mentioned
earlier, the separation

00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:09.020
of storage and
compute would not be

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:12.440
possible without the evolution
of networks, which increased

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:15.170
the speed at which compute
clusters can pull data

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:18.350
from storage and reduce latency.

00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:20.660
Just like Colossus,
Google Cloud services

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:23.930
like BigQuery and Spanner
leverage the same network

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:27.710
that powers the underpinnings
of Google's systems.

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:29.930
And we call this
network Jupiter.

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:33.440
So Jupiter is made up of
an in-house custom network,

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:36.020
hardware and software,
and it connects

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:39.140
all of the servers in our
data centers together,

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:44.860
powering our distributed
computing and storage systems.

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:47.530
So we discussed our file
system and the network

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:50.260
that connects the storage
and compute together.

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:53.380
Now, leading all the
operations in our data center

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:57.070
is Borg, Google's internal
cluster management system

00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:59.770
and the predecessor
to Kubernetes.

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:02.800
So Borg goes ahead and
provisions the needed compute

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:06.700
resources to handle workloads
for Spanner and BigQuery,

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:09.070
running hundreds of
thousands of jobs

00:04:09.070 --> 00:04:11.560
and making computing
much more efficient.

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:13.380
This allows our
data centers to run

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:16.459
at a really high utilization.

00:04:16.459 --> 00:04:18.760
So now, let's see how
these pieces come together

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:20.169
throughout the
life of your data.

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:26.380
So let's take a look at
Cymbal Gaming, our fake gaming

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:30.430
company who uses Spanner to
support transactional or OLT

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:31.780
workloads.

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:34.900
So a new user might log
into the Spanner game

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:37.100
and enter their information.

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:39.970
This data is then sent
to Colossus for storage,

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:43.300
but it's going to be in
Spanner's file format.

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:46.960
Next, this data is requested in
a query sent through Spanner's

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:48.250
compute engine.

00:04:48.250 --> 00:04:50.050
And finally, the
results of the query

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:52.240
will be returned
in the application

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:54.460
to populate the new
user's profile when

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.240
they load the page.

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:58.750
So Spanner's storage
and compute are both

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:01.990
going to be optimized for
those transactional workloads.

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:06.480
And Derek is going to talk
more about that in a minute.

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:08.870
So even though Cymbal
is using Spanner

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:11.390
for transactional
workloads, they also

00:05:11.390 --> 00:05:13.880
want to perform analytics
on this information

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:17.090
to understand how users are
actually playing their game.

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:18.980
But for analytical
workloads, they're

00:05:18.980 --> 00:05:21.590
going to instead use BigQuery.

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:26.070
So once the user's data lands in
Spanner, they have two options.

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:29.390
First, they can replicate the
data in BigQuery's storage

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:32.780
by pushing new rows into
the native BigQuery table.

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:34.910
This is going to provide
optimized storage

00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:36.950
for analytics.

00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:39.530
On the other hand, they
can use a federated query

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:41.840
sent from BigQuery
over to Spanner

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:44.510
so that they get real-time
access to information

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:46.970
from their application.

00:05:46.970 --> 00:05:49.900
Either way, they'll benefit
from BigQuery's query engine

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:52.540
to perform scalable
analysis, including

00:05:52.540 --> 00:05:54.640
built-in machine
learning capabilities

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:56.980
that power business
intelligence reporting

00:05:56.980 --> 00:06:01.490
and real decision-making
from business users.

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:04.450
So now that you understand how
BigQuery and Spanner both come

00:06:04.450 --> 00:06:06.590
together to support
your data needs,

00:06:06.590 --> 00:06:08.530
let's talk about how
Spanner is actually

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:11.110
optimized for those
transactional workloads.

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:13.037
So, Derek, over to you.

00:06:13.037 --> 00:06:13.870
DEREK DOWNEY: Great.

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:15.160
Thanks, Leigha.

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:18.100
Cloud Spanner is Google's fully
managed distributed relational

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:19.090
database.

00:06:19.090 --> 00:06:22.030
Spanner provides customers with
minimal operational overhead

00:06:22.030 --> 00:06:24.010
with no downtime
for schema changes,

00:06:24.010 --> 00:06:26.050
or even maintenance windows.

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:28.630
This allows Spanner to
provide an industry leading

00:06:28.630 --> 00:06:30.810
99.999% SLA.

00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:33.240
Because Spanner is
distributed database,

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:35.970
it provides seamless
replication and scaling

00:06:35.970 --> 00:06:39.740
across regions in Google Cloud.

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:42.200
Spanner processes over
one billion requests

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:46.040
per second peak, and all of
that as a relational database

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:49.670
that provides strong external
consistency, made possible

00:06:49.670 --> 00:06:51.950
by the technologies
mentioned earlier,

00:06:51.950 --> 00:06:55.190
Borg, Colossus, and Jupiter.

00:06:55.190 --> 00:06:57.860
To set up an example,
imagine that Cymbal Games

00:06:57.860 --> 00:07:00.590
has developed a game that
will be played by people

00:07:00.590 --> 00:07:02.160
all around the world.

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:04.850
These players must be able
to sign up, authenticate,

00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:06.170
and play the game.

00:07:06.170 --> 00:07:09.320
Players really don't like
to have to redo actions,

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:12.350
so this activity has to
have traditional consistency

00:07:12.350 --> 00:07:13.820
guarantees.

00:07:13.820 --> 00:07:16.460
Another thing players
don't like is downtime.

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:20.450
If they can't play, they get
upset, and it better be fast.

00:07:20.450 --> 00:07:24.260
So Cymbal Games will need to
have the ability to scale up

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:26.320
to meet user demand.

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:29.810
With availability, consistency,
and scalability in mind,

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:32.425
let's see how Cloud Spanner can
help them achieve their goals.

00:07:35.030 --> 00:07:36.920
When you create a
Spanner node, you

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:40.640
get access to Spanner resources
in three or more zones.

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:42.260
Zones are Borg cells.

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:44.900
These cells are a collection
of Spanner resources

00:07:44.900 --> 00:07:47.960
that are physically
separate from other cells.

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:51.050
Spanner resources consist of
Spanner servers and Colossus

00:07:51.050 --> 00:07:55.460
allocations of data storage,
as discussed previously.

00:07:55.460 --> 00:07:57.860
Spanner servers provide
the compute capacity

00:07:57.860 --> 00:08:00.890
for your cluster, and they
contain the span process,

00:08:00.890 --> 00:08:05.360
Paxos, and other board tasks
to enable Google SREs to manage

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:07.640
the environment.

00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:10.610
The zones can be within a
single region, as shown here,

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:12.770
or across multiple regions.

00:08:12.770 --> 00:08:14.810
If you have a regional
configuration,

00:08:14.810 --> 00:08:16.460
you have zones
close together that

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:18.800
provide the lowest read
and write latencies,

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:22.880
but you only get an SLA
of 99.99% availability.

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:24.920
In a multi-regional
configuration,

00:08:24.920 --> 00:08:27.170
your zones are located
farther apart, which

00:08:27.170 --> 00:08:29.870
can increase latencies,
but it provides

00:08:29.870 --> 00:08:34.570
global scalability and an
SLA of 99.999% availability.

00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:38.260
Now, imagine that Cymbal
Games has a table of users.

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:42.460
The user data is stored on
Colossus and encrypted at rest.

00:08:42.460 --> 00:08:45.820
A copy of this data is
in at least three zones,

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:48.760
and replication of the rights
is handled synchronously

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:50.680
by Paxos consensus.

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:52.930
So their data will
survive zone outages

00:08:52.930 --> 00:09:00.030
and allows for zero downtime
maintenance as well.

00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:05.100
To understand consistency, let's
go over typical transaction

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:06.970
life cycles.

00:09:06.970 --> 00:09:09.400
When a new user signs
up, Cymbal Games

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:12.400
issues an insert
statement to Spanner.

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:14.770
Since nodes exist
in multiple zones,

00:09:14.770 --> 00:09:17.050
where the insert
statement hits depends

00:09:17.050 --> 00:09:20.530
on which zone is the
current Paxos leader.

00:09:20.530 --> 00:09:23.770
In this example,
zone 2 is our leader.

00:09:23.770 --> 00:09:26.080
A single endpoint is
exposed to the client

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:29.830
and Spanner internals
handles routing of the query.

00:09:29.830 --> 00:09:31.390
When the leader
receives the write,

00:09:31.390 --> 00:09:35.470
it acquires locks and generates
a true time commit time stamp,

00:09:35.470 --> 00:09:37.510
then, it sends the
write to other nodes

00:09:37.510 --> 00:09:39.550
in the configuration.

00:09:39.550 --> 00:09:41.890
These nodes write the
mutation to something

00:09:41.890 --> 00:09:44.650
like a durable transaction
log and confirm the write

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:46.210
to the leader.

00:09:46.210 --> 00:09:48.580
After a quorum of nodes
approves the write,

00:09:48.580 --> 00:09:51.190
then the leader tells the
nodes to commit the data,

00:09:51.190 --> 00:09:53.650
it releases the locks,
and returns the success

00:09:53.650 --> 00:09:54.940
to the client.

00:09:54.940 --> 00:10:00.580
All of this usually happens
within a few milliseconds.

00:10:00.580 --> 00:10:02.230
We can't do a
session about Spanner

00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:04.210
without mentioning TrueTime.

00:10:04.210 --> 00:10:06.940
TrueTime is a highly
available internal service

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:10.480
backed by a network of
atomic and GPS masters.

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:13.360
This service can be called
to generate a guaranteed

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:15.340
monotonically
increasing timestamp

00:10:15.340 --> 00:10:19.360
with microsecond granularity
across all nodes.

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:22.840
In Spanner, these timestamps
are generated for writes.

00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:25.000
It is precisely
because of TrueTime

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:27.520
commit time stamps that
allows a distributed

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:31.910
database like Spanner to ensure
strong external consistency.

00:10:31.910 --> 00:10:33.775
So let's see how they
are used for reads.

00:10:36.710 --> 00:10:38.900
Once a user account
has been created,

00:10:38.900 --> 00:10:41.520
they'd want to be able to
log in and start playing.

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:43.850
So Cymbal Games needs
a consistent read

00:10:43.850 --> 00:10:46.560
to provide authentication
for the user.

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:48.200
In this example,
the read is going

00:10:48.200 --> 00:10:51.140
to be executed against
zone 1 since it's

00:10:51.140 --> 00:10:53.980
closest to the client
making the request.

00:10:53.980 --> 00:10:56.620
But since zone 2 is
the leader, zone 1

00:10:56.620 --> 00:10:59.880
checks in to make sure it
has the latest commit data.

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:03.090
If the data is the latest, it
can be returned to the client.

00:11:03.090 --> 00:11:06.840
Otherwise, the read waits until
the node receives updates.

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:09.630
How does the replica know
that it has the latest data?

00:11:09.630 --> 00:11:11.820
With TrueTime
commit time stamps.

00:11:11.820 --> 00:11:14.100
The replica compares
its own TrueTime state

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:18.570
with the latest commit time
stamp to know if it's enough

00:11:18.570 --> 00:11:20.055
up to date to serve the read.

00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:26.670
But not all reads of the user
need to be strongly consistent.

00:11:26.670 --> 00:11:30.030
Since user data doesn't change
that often, Cymbal Games can

00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:31.860
choose to issue a
time-bound read,

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:34.460
otherwise known as a still read.

00:11:34.460 --> 00:11:36.700
To do this, they
provide the max age

00:11:36.700 --> 00:11:40.210
that is acceptable for their
query, making it time-bound.

00:11:40.210 --> 00:11:42.580
This allows the Spanner
node closest to the client

00:11:42.580 --> 00:11:45.760
to respond to the query without
incurring additional round

00:11:45.760 --> 00:11:49.420
trip latency, communicating
with the Paxos leader.

00:11:49.420 --> 00:11:51.220
No matter how fast
the Jupiter network

00:11:51.220 --> 00:11:54.340
is, if you can cut out latency
across zones, you should.

00:11:57.450 --> 00:11:59.610
With availability and
consistency covered,

00:11:59.610 --> 00:12:02.610
Cymbal Games still needs
scalability out of Spanner.

00:12:02.610 --> 00:12:05.110
So let's look at that.

00:12:05.110 --> 00:12:08.710
As a first step, Cymbal
Games gets closer to launch,

00:12:08.710 --> 00:12:10.840
they can go ahead and
increase the number of nodes

00:12:10.840 --> 00:12:13.540
from pre-prod levels
to prod levels simply

00:12:13.540 --> 00:12:15.640
by updating the Cloud console.

00:12:19.190 --> 00:12:21.830
Next, as the user
data grows, Spanner

00:12:21.830 --> 00:12:24.290
will start to create
splits of the data.

00:12:24.290 --> 00:12:26.840
It does this both by
detecting CPU load

00:12:26.840 --> 00:12:29.330
increases and hot spots.

00:12:29.330 --> 00:12:32.240
And we'll split the data into
chunks to address these issues.

00:12:34.810 --> 00:12:37.540
These splits can be hosted
on different Spanner servers

00:12:37.540 --> 00:12:40.180
backed by different
Colossus allocations, which

00:12:40.180 --> 00:12:42.670
helps distribute the load.

00:12:42.670 --> 00:12:45.460
Data splits and increasing
the number of Spanner nodes

00:12:45.460 --> 00:12:47.620
gives Cymbal Games
the ability to scale

00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:49.600
as much as they need to.

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:51.580
But to avoid headaches
down the road,

00:12:51.580 --> 00:12:55.120
they need to be sure to place
related data in the same split

00:12:55.120 --> 00:12:57.010
using interleaving.

00:12:57.010 --> 00:13:02.650
Imagine user activity data
that relates to the user data.

00:13:02.650 --> 00:13:05.470
By interleaving the user
activity table with the user

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:08.290
table, Spanner will
change the splitting logic

00:13:08.290 --> 00:13:12.620
to make sure that the data
stays together on Colossus.

00:13:12.620 --> 00:13:14.960
In this way, Spanner
scales easily

00:13:14.960 --> 00:13:17.030
while also keeping
performance high

00:13:17.030 --> 00:13:20.090
by avoiding additional locking
required to read or write

00:13:20.090 --> 00:13:22.420
across multiple splits.

00:13:22.420 --> 00:13:24.220
Managing this
distributed database

00:13:24.220 --> 00:13:26.650
for transactional
workloads is only possible

00:13:26.650 --> 00:13:29.980
because of technologies like
TrueTime, Borg, Colossus,

00:13:29.980 --> 00:13:31.120
and Jupiter.

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:33.280
So now, Leigha is going
to take it over and talk

00:13:33.280 --> 00:13:35.655
about analytical workloads.

00:13:35.655 --> 00:13:36.530
LEIGHA JARETT: Great.

00:13:36.530 --> 00:13:37.670
Thanks, Derek.

00:13:37.670 --> 00:13:41.900
So now that you understand how
Spanner is great and optimized

00:13:41.900 --> 00:13:43.880
for transactional
workloads, let's

00:13:43.880 --> 00:13:46.280
talk about how BigQuery's
architecture supports

00:13:46.280 --> 00:13:48.570
analytical workloads.

00:13:48.570 --> 00:13:50.780
So let's start by
adding the new user

00:13:50.780 --> 00:13:53.930
data into a native
BigQuery user table.

00:13:53.930 --> 00:13:57.020
As soon as you push data
into a native BigQuery table,

00:13:57.020 --> 00:14:00.350
the data is going to be
transformed into a file format

00:14:00.350 --> 00:14:01.940
called Capacitor.

00:14:01.940 --> 00:14:05.910
Capacitor uses what we call
columnar-oriented storage,

00:14:05.910 --> 00:14:07.760
meaning, each
column in the table

00:14:07.760 --> 00:14:10.700
is going to be stored in a
different area of the file.

00:14:10.700 --> 00:14:12.950
So the amount of the
columns you query

00:14:12.950 --> 00:14:15.260
is going to be proportional
to the amount of data

00:14:15.260 --> 00:14:17.150
that's actually scanned.

00:14:17.150 --> 00:14:20.090
Now, if you think about it,
this is optimal for analytics

00:14:20.090 --> 00:14:23.870
because you're often aggregating
just a few columns over lots

00:14:23.870 --> 00:14:26.050
and lots of different rows.

00:14:26.050 --> 00:14:29.140
Plus, each column
also has independently

00:14:29.140 --> 00:14:31.120
compressed information.

00:14:31.120 --> 00:14:33.190
Here, we use different
types of encoding

00:14:33.190 --> 00:14:36.490
to optimize storage, which
makes it easier for Dremel,

00:14:36.490 --> 00:14:40.330
our query engine, to quickly
find and use the data it needs

00:14:40.330 --> 00:14:41.920
for your query.

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:45.130
Even better, you can choose
to cluster or partition

00:14:45.130 --> 00:14:48.550
your data stored in Capacitor
so that Dremel can easily

00:14:48.550 --> 00:14:53.670
eliminate certain files
or blocks from the query.

00:14:53.670 --> 00:14:55.590
Let's look at a quick
example of this.

00:14:55.590 --> 00:14:57.980
So let's say we
have our user table

00:14:57.980 --> 00:15:00.680
and we decided to
partition based on the date

00:15:00.680 --> 00:15:02.600
that the user was created.

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:07.310
We're also clustering or sorting
our table based on the game ID.

00:15:07.310 --> 00:15:10.880
So now, when we filter our data
based on created date and game

00:15:10.880 --> 00:15:14.780
ID, Dremel is going to use
the file headings in Capacitor

00:15:14.780 --> 00:15:17.780
to reject the entire file
or file block if it's not

00:15:17.780 --> 00:15:19.310
needed for the query.

00:15:19.310 --> 00:15:22.070
This means that less data
is going to be scanned

00:15:22.070 --> 00:15:24.920
and your query is going to
be both cheaper and faster.

00:15:27.500 --> 00:15:30.430
So in Colossus, your data
is stored in Capacitor,

00:15:30.430 --> 00:15:33.130
and it's going to be
automatically compressed,

00:15:33.130 --> 00:15:36.400
encrypted, replicated,
and distributed.

00:15:36.400 --> 00:15:40.420
So 100% of your data is going
to be encrypted at rest, just

00:15:40.420 --> 00:15:43.150
like Derek mentioned
with Spanner.

00:15:43.150 --> 00:15:46.630
And Colossus is also going
to ensure durability using

00:15:46.630 --> 00:15:48.910
something called
erasure encoding, which

00:15:48.910 --> 00:15:53.420
stores redundant chunks of data
on multiple physical disks.

00:15:53.420 --> 00:15:56.660
So immediately, upon
writing data to Colossus,

00:15:56.660 --> 00:15:59.780
BigQuery is going to start
this geo-replication process,

00:15:59.780 --> 00:16:02.030
mirroring all of the
data into different data

00:16:02.030 --> 00:16:05.300
centers around the specified
region or multi-region,

00:16:05.300 --> 00:16:08.980
depending on how you set
up your BigQuery data set.

00:16:08.980 --> 00:16:11.190
This means that you have
backups of your data stored

00:16:11.190 --> 00:16:16.150
in case of disk failure
or data center outages.

00:16:16.150 --> 00:16:18.820
OK, so we talked a little
bit about BigQuery storage.

00:16:18.820 --> 00:16:21.240
Now, let's talk about compute.

00:16:21.240 --> 00:16:23.370
So Dremel, BigQuery's
query engine,

00:16:23.370 --> 00:16:25.770
is made up of
something called slots,

00:16:25.770 --> 00:16:29.670
and these are Google's
proprietary units of CPU, RAM,

00:16:29.670 --> 00:16:33.570
and network, and they're used
to execute the SQL queries.

00:16:33.570 --> 00:16:35.820
So BigQuery will
automatically calculate

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:38.460
how many slots are
required by each query,

00:16:38.460 --> 00:16:41.610
depending on the data size and
the complexity of the query

00:16:41.610 --> 00:16:43.080
itself.

00:16:43.080 --> 00:16:45.270
You can think of a
slot as a worker,

00:16:45.270 --> 00:16:48.330
and slots are going to be
responsible for executing parts

00:16:48.330 --> 00:16:51.360
of the active query,
independently and in parallel,

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:54.120
and they're going to perform
partial aggregations.

00:16:54.120 --> 00:16:57.520
They'll also read and
write data back to storage.

00:16:57.520 --> 00:17:00.360
So the awesome thing about
BigQuery's architecture

00:17:00.360 --> 00:17:03.870
is that you can actually
purchase slots and reserve them

00:17:03.870 --> 00:17:07.000
for certain workloads
in your organization.

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:09.119
This means that you can
scale your throughput

00:17:09.119 --> 00:17:10.515
and your runtime as needed.

00:17:13.030 --> 00:17:17.130
So now, let's go a little bit
deeper into query execution.

00:17:17.130 --> 00:17:19.460
First, when you submit
a query, BigQuery

00:17:19.460 --> 00:17:22.170
is going to break it down into
different stages, which will

00:17:22.170 --> 00:17:24.910
be processed by those slots.

00:17:24.910 --> 00:17:27.310
Intermediary results are
going to be written back

00:17:27.310 --> 00:17:30.130
to what we call the
remote memory shuffle.

00:17:30.130 --> 00:17:32.560
Since data here is
usually stored in memory,

00:17:32.560 --> 00:17:35.320
it's really fast for slots
to read and write data

00:17:35.320 --> 00:17:37.490
from the shuffle.

00:17:37.490 --> 00:17:40.610
Now, each shuffled row
can be consumed by slots

00:17:40.610 --> 00:17:42.770
as soon as it's created.

00:17:42.770 --> 00:17:46.190
This makes it possible to
execute distributed operations

00:17:46.190 --> 00:17:48.350
in a parallel
pipeline, which means

00:17:48.350 --> 00:17:52.700
that we can analyze huge
amounts of data really fast.

00:17:52.700 --> 00:17:56.210
And if a worker were to have an
issue during query processing,

00:17:56.210 --> 00:17:58.880
another worker could
actually simply pick up

00:17:58.880 --> 00:18:00.860
from where the
previous one left off

00:18:00.860 --> 00:18:04.100
because they're able to read
from the previous shuffle.

00:18:04.100 --> 00:18:06.740
So this adds an extra
layer of resilience

00:18:06.740 --> 00:18:08.990
to failures within the
workers themselves.

00:18:11.650 --> 00:18:13.570
OK, so let's say
we're running a query

00:18:13.570 --> 00:18:16.660
to count how many users
joined Cymbal Games yesterday

00:18:16.660 --> 00:18:19.880
before feeding them
into our ML model.

00:18:19.880 --> 00:18:22.010
In the first stage
of the query, slots

00:18:22.010 --> 00:18:26.080
will read the columnar files
from Colossus in parallel.

00:18:26.080 --> 00:18:30.310
Each slot processes a column or
file block, filters the data,

00:18:30.310 --> 00:18:34.860
and then writes the partial
aggregations to the shuffle.

00:18:34.860 --> 00:18:38.160
In the next stage, a slot
reads the data from the shuffle

00:18:38.160 --> 00:18:41.580
and combines those partial
aggregations together.

00:18:41.580 --> 00:18:44.550
Then, it's going to write
the results back to Colossus

00:18:44.550 --> 00:18:47.655
so that it can be served as a
cache table back to our user.

00:18:50.640 --> 00:18:54.020
So how do these architectural
distinctions of BigQuery

00:18:54.020 --> 00:18:57.380
actually translate to
business value for you?

00:18:57.380 --> 00:18:59.300
Well, first of all,
we talked about how

00:18:59.300 --> 00:19:02.090
BigQuery's optimized
storage and compute allows

00:19:02.090 --> 00:19:05.660
for super fast queries
over huge data volumes,

00:19:05.660 --> 00:19:07.310
meaning your business
will be empowered

00:19:07.310 --> 00:19:11.740
to make informed decisions
without ever missing a beat.

00:19:11.740 --> 00:19:13.660
Next, we showed
how BigQuery helps

00:19:13.660 --> 00:19:16.540
you save money on data
storage and analysis

00:19:16.540 --> 00:19:18.820
so you spend less
resources answering

00:19:18.820 --> 00:19:21.920
business-critical questions.

00:19:21.920 --> 00:19:24.650
We also talked about how
BigQuery's storage ensures

00:19:24.650 --> 00:19:27.230
reliability and
durability, meaning

00:19:27.230 --> 00:19:28.940
there's a very
low risk of losing

00:19:28.940 --> 00:19:33.520
information and interrupting
business processes.

00:19:33.520 --> 00:19:36.810
And finally, the ability to
purchase and dedicate slots

00:19:36.810 --> 00:19:40.590
to specific workloads means you
can scale throughput and run

00:19:40.590 --> 00:19:43.590
time so that you continue
to support your business

00:19:43.590 --> 00:19:49.290
analytics as your data
and your use cases grow.

00:19:49.290 --> 00:19:51.240
And don't just take
our word for it.

00:19:51.240 --> 00:19:54.630
Google Cloud actually has
hundreds of BigQuery customers

00:19:54.630 --> 00:19:59.400
with petabyte scale
data warehouses.

00:19:59.400 --> 00:20:02.190
So thanks, Derek, for
walking us through Spanner.

00:20:02.190 --> 00:20:04.530
And thank you all so
much for joining us today

00:20:04.530 --> 00:20:07.830
to dig into Spanner and
BigQuery under the hood.

00:20:07.830 --> 00:20:10.110
Here, we provided
some resources to help

00:20:10.110 --> 00:20:12.990
you get started using
both Spanner and BigQuery,

00:20:12.990 --> 00:20:14.850
and I've also
